# What survives on a small team's rig: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers with cheap hardware and little data

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Most of the robotic papers are either tested in sim or on expensive rigs. From our tested papers, {{rig_far}} out of {{papers}} were done in simulation or on rigs above $20k, and only {{rig_lt5k}} on rigs under $5k. But the question is, does a claim still validate for smaller teams with cheaper hardware, less data and shorter tasks? This is what we want to answer in this paper. For that we re-tested {{c_paper}} claims out of {{papers}} papers on our cheap hardware, an Nvidia Jetson Orin NX 16GB and a robot arm SO-101 (about €{{rig_eur_round}}). We used the same protocol for each claim: a bar fixed before any testing, and the cheapest baseline tested first. Our results: {{c_paper_repro}} claims hold, {{t_c_auth}} fail using the paper's method, {{t_c_var}} fail on our variation, {{t_c_eng}} on our inference engine and {{t_c_scope}} on a task outside the paper's regime. Most of the failures come as much from the papers' methods as from our short tasks, small data and our own variants, and for each we explain why. The code, data and all numbers are public and can be replicated.

## 1. Introduction

Out of the {{papers}} papers we tested, {{rig_far}} of them are done in simulation or on expensive rigs above $20k, and only {{rig_lt5k}} on rigs under $5k. This means small teams can either trust the paper or re-test the claim on their own hardware. Both of them are costly, either when validating or debugging if it does not work. The question really is, do the papers' claims still hold on cheap hardware, with little data and short tasks? And this is the environment small teams or solo founders have.

To answer this question, we re-tested each claim on our own cheap rig. We used the same testing protocol each time: a bar fixed before any test, and the cheapest baseline (low cost or free) run first on the same data, to filter out claims early before investing in them.

Out of the {{c_paper}} claims, only {{c_paper_repro}} hold on our rig. The failures are split between the paper's method failing ({{t_c_auth}}), our variant of the method failing ({{t_c_var}}), our inference engine ({{t_c_eng}}) and one outside the paper's regime. But, and it is important to say it again, a fail on our rig does not mean the paper is wrong: it just does not work on our specific setup and data.

## 2. Rig and data

Here's the rig we used for re-tests:

- A robot arm SO-101: 5 joints + gripper, 1 wrist camera and 1 front-top camera
- Leader + follower arm: all teleoperation done using the leader arm
- A Nvidia Jetson Orin NX 16GB: runs the inference
- Cloud GPU instances for training only

The estimated cost of the whole rig is about €{{rig_eur_round}}, plus under ${{cost_round}} of cloud GPU (for all re-tests).

Here's the data we used:

The teleoperated data (HF dataset: so101_bowls_all_trimmed):

- 2 short tasks: put one bowl into another
- {{tel_eps}} episodes, {{tel_frames}} frames at {{tel_fps}} fps, about {{tel_min}} min
- split: {{tel_train}} episodes in the train set, {{tel_val}} in the validation set
- mostly used for the world models and the VLA policies

Some policy rollouts:

- {{ro_eps}} episodes: {{ro_succ}} successes, {{ro_fail}} failures, labelled by hand by the operator
- main set for most monitor tests: {{ro_p_eps}} episodes ({{ro_p_succ}} successes / {{ro_p_fail}} failures), leaving out the episodes of one policy family (TurboVLA's)
- FAIL-Detect / SAFE: the {{ro_succ}} successes split in half, {{ro_half}} to calibrate and {{ro_half}} to test, over 20 random splits
- a separate set of 88 of our own episodes, top camera only, to check the reward model directly on our rig

The ArmnetBench dataset (public, HF dataset: armnet/armnetbench_v01_lerobot_so101) [@2607.24481]: {{arm_n}} labelled SO-101 rollouts from {{arm_pol}} policies, used for the reward models and judges.

The timing set:

- {{tim_eps}} recorded episodes
- {{tim_b}} chunk boundaries

This is used for analysing and measuring chunking and latency.

But this data has limits that explain some of the failures:

- Short tasks only: each episode is about {{tel_ep_s}} s
- No multi-step, memory-based or long-horizon tasks
- Everything has been done in one place, with one robot and one task family, which means this may not translate to real-world usage of such tasks.
- All the rollout outcomes have been judged by one human, so they are subjective (not blind).

## 3. What we tested and how

There are 4 kinds of papers we picked for re-tests (see Table 1 for details):

- World models ({{f_wm}} papers): the goal is to learn a latent from the cameras to predict or plan. In this case, the question is "does the latent beat a free signal?"
- Failure monitors and judges ({{f_mon}} papers): the goal is to detect if an episode fails, or how far it has progressed. In this case, the question is "does the monitor beat a trivial signal?"
- VLA policies ({{f_vla}} papers): the goal is to follow and execute an instruction on the arm.
- Chunking and edge inference ({{f_chunk}} papers): the goal is to improve how action chunks are made and joined, by making it smoother or faster.

All the papers were chosen because we judged that they could reasonably run on our rig, which filters out many papers from the get-go.

We used 2 rules to re-test the claims.

Rule 1: the bar to achieve to validate the claim needs to be written from the paper and its code, not after running the claim once. This avoids contaminating the bar with our own results. Where the paper gives a number, the bar comes from it; otherwise the bar is ours.

Rule 2: we create and launch the cheapest baseline first, on the same data. It is built from cheap things that can be used on our tasks, like the last frame, the joint values or the episode length. If the method loses to it, the bar is not useful. For example, does a failure monitor catch failures better than directly checking the episode length?

There are only 4 verdicts possible for each re-test: holds, meaning the bar is met; fails, so the bar is missed; not proven, because we cannot decide considering our rig and context; and not tested, not run yet (because of time to test, cost, ...). A paper is considered failed if any of its claims fails. We decided that because, for us, a paper that is only half successful is considered failed. All the failures can be categorized in 4 categories:

- the paper's method failed
- our variant of the method failed (because we could not use the paper's method directly)
- the limitation of our inference engine
- or a task that goes outside the paper's scope

We had 2 cases of weaker bar: for SmolVLA and VLA-JEPA, we had no numeric bar, so instead we launched 10 trials judged by a human. 9 out of 10 were a success, which means a true rate anywhere from {{vla_lo}} to {{vla_hi}} (Wilson interval, 95%).

Table 1: The four kinds of papers, what we test in each, the cheapest baseline we build for it, and the verdicts.

| kind of paper | papers | what we test | cheapest baseline | verdicts |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| world models | {{f_wm}} | predict the next state; rank or plan actions | copy-last (next state = current state); the commanded joints or the actions alone | {{f_wm_v}} |
| failure monitors and judges | {{f_mon}} | catch failures; track progress | episode length; the same model with shuffled actions | {{f_mon_v}} |
| VLA policies | {{f_vla}} | succeed on our arm | none: success over 10 trials judged by the operator, no numeric bar fixed before | {{f_vla_v}} |
| chunking and edge inference | {{f_chunk}} | smoother joins between chunks; time per chunk | the same policy without the trick; the real-time budget on the Orin | {{f_chunk_v}} |

## 4. Results

Table 2 and Figure 1 give the verdicts, and Table 1 breaks them down by kind of paper. Of the {{c_paper}} claims, {{c_paper_repro}} hold, {{c_not_proven}} are not proven and {{c_not_tested}} are not tested yet. Of the {{c_paper_fail}} that fail, {{t_c_auth}} fail with the authors' method on our data, {{t_c_var}} in our own variant, {{t_c_eng}} because of our engine and {{t_c_scope}} on a task outside the paper's regime. Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} reproduce, and {{t_p_auth}} fail with the authors' method. D-JEPA is counted as not tested: we ran it, but only with proxy labels, not with the success labels the paper uses.

The pattern by kind looks sharp, with all {{f_mon}} failure monitors and judges failing and all {{f_vla}} VLA policies reproducing, but neither half is strong evidence. Most of the monitor failures are in our own variants, and two of the three VLA results rest on 10 trials with no numeric bar. The appendix gives every paper with its claim, its bar and our result.

![Fig. 1 — the {{c_paper}} paper claims by verdict: {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce, {{t_c_auth}} fail with the authors' method on our data, {{t_c_other}} fail only in our variant of the method, because of our inference engine or outside the paper's regime, and the rest are open.](fig/verdicts.png)

Table 2: Verdicts by paper and by claim. A paper fails if any of its claims fails; failures are split by whose method failed.

| verdict | papers | claims |
|---|---|---|
| reproduces | {{p_reproduces}} | {{c_reproduces}} |
| fails, authors' method | {{t_p_auth}} | {{t_c_auth}} |
| fails, our variant | {{t_p_var}} | {{t_c_var}} |
| fails, our engine | {{t_p_eng}} | {{t_c_eng}} |
| fails, outside the paper's regime | {{t_p_scope}} | {{t_c_scope}} |
| not proven | {{p_not_proven}} | {{c_not_proven}} |
| not tested | {{p_not_tested}} | {{c_not_tested}} |

## 5. The cheapest baseline

The cheapest baseline is the signal we get for free on the same data as the method (Table 1). It needs no training and runs in minutes. We compared {{b_n}} paper claims with such a baseline (Table 3). The method clearly beat it in {{b_beat}} cases, beat it by less than its bar in {{b_under}}, and lost to it in {{b_lost}}. We ran a paired bootstrap over episodes (2,000 resamples, 95 % percentile interval, protocol frozen before any number) on the {{bs_have}} of the {{b_n}} rows whose per-episode rows were stored or could be written again by re-running the evaluation. {{bs_loses}} losses have their interval below zero: LeWorldModel against the actions alone, at every target distance we tested, ContactGuard, which is our variant, and Robometer, which comes from how the dataset ends its episodes (below). The interval of adaptive chunking contains zero, and HWM, PACT-VLA and REMORA beat their baselines. For {{bs_nomatch}} rows, among them DINO-WM against the actions alone, the re-run gave back the stored number only to the second or third decimal, so by our protocol we did not bootstrap them; for RoboJEPA the encoder of the original run is not on our machine. LeWorldModel's loss is therefore the one clean case of a method losing to a free baseline with the authors' method on our data; DINO-WM, on the same test, points the same way but has no interval.

The choice of baseline decides the verdict, even for a single model. LeWorldModel predicts the next latent far better than copy-last, with an error of {{b_lewm_pred}} against {{b_lewm_cl}}, and that claim holds. Yet the same model ranks the chunk that was really executed worse than the actions alone do, {{ex_c_ours}} against {{ex_c_base}}, and that claim fails. Predicting well is not the same as planning well, and testing only one of the two would give the wrong picture.

The same baseline is also not equally strong on all data. On ArmnetBench, episode length beats the reward model ({{ex_b_len}} against {{ex_b_ours}}), because there a failed episode tends to run until the time limit, so the length gives the label away. On our own 88 episodes, where failures do not run to the time limit, length is close to chance ({{b_len_own}}). What a judge must beat therefore depends on who stopped the episode, and the baseline has to be computed on one's own data.

Copy-last and the joints are strong partly because our task is short and simple: little moves apart from the arm, and the arm's state is already in the joints. On long tasks with many moving objects a learned latent has more to add, and we did not test that. Some papers report a cheap baseline themselves (shuffled actions for ReAP, a random pick for PACT-VLA, net joint displacement for HWM), and on our data those methods beat it too; Robometer reports no episode-length baseline. In practice, the baseline is the first thing to run: if the method loses to it, one can stop there, and only if it wins is the bar worth checking.

Table 3: The paper claims we compared with a cheap baseline, on the same data.

| method | baseline (what is measured) | method | baseline | outcome | paired difference, positive = method better [95 % interval] |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | copy-last (latent error at 30 steps, lower is better) | 0.31 | 0.89 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0011}} |
| LeWorldModel | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.60 | 0.68–0.90 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0012}} |
| DINO-WM | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.61 | 0.68–0.90 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0021}} |
| RoboJEPA | copy-last (9-step latent error) | 11.8 % below copy-last | – | beat, under its bar of 15 % | {{bs_ci_c0031}} |
| ContactGuard | the current latent (failure AUROC) | 0.70 | 0.82 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0061}} |
| ReAP | shuffled actions (failure AUROC) | 0.789 | 0.708 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0081}} |
| Robometer | episode length (failure AUROC, ArmnetBench) | 0.818 | 0.938 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0102}} |
| HWM | net joint displacement (cosine to the macro-action) | 0.608 | 0.551 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0162}} |
| Adaptive chunking | fixed chunk length (boundary jump, lower is better) | 0.973 | 0.940 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0192}} |
| PACT-VLA | a random pick (error of the executed window) | −0.27° against random | – | beat | {{bs_ci_c0201}} |
| REMORA | per-frame readout (readout flips) | 0.70 × per-frame | – | beat, under its bar of 0.5 × | {{bs_ci_c0221}} |

## 6. A testing order

The failures point to three checks, in this order. First, time the method on the target device: adaptive chunking needs {{ex_a_ours}} for one decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}, partly because our engine samples without batching, and this is the cheapest failure to find. Second, compare it with the cheapest baseline on one's own data: this is where the world models fail, since the commanded joints already hold the arm's state on a short task, and it is also where a dataset can mislead, as episode length does on ArmnetBench. Only then is it worth checking the method against its bar. Of the three, only the learned-latent pattern rests on more than one case, so we offer this as a working order, not as a taxonomy of how methods fail.

## 7. Why they failed here: the method, our data, or our setup

For each failure, Table 4 says whose method failed and what on our side may explain it. Most failures have at least one such cause, so a failure here is a failure on this rig, with this data, and not a verdict on the method in general.

Our data is small and simple. We have {{tel_train}} training episodes of one short task, about {{tel_ep_s}} s each, where the papers use hours of varied data; HWM, for instance, uses about 130 h against our 0.6 h. On a short task with one arm, the commanded joints already hold most of the state, which leaves little for a latent to add. The task is also easy for our policy, since SmolVLA is at its ceiling on it, so there is little uncertainty for an entropy rule to read: the chunk length chosen by adaptive chunking sat at its minimum in 86 % of decisions. For H-JEPA, our task lies outside the regime the paper targets, as there is no gap between slow and fast parts of the scene, so we count its failure as out of scope rather than a failure of the method.

We also have too few failures. Our policies mostly succeed, and the main rollout set has only {{ro_p_fail}} failures in {{ro_p_eps}} episodes. A bar such as five failures caught out of eight is coarse, and a single episode can change the verdict (ReAP, VLA-Corrector, SURVEYOR).

In {{t_p_var}} papers our version of the method is not exactly theirs. For ContactGuard we used mixed policies and two cameras, where the paper uses one policy and three cameras. For FAIL-Detect and SAFE we used our world model's surprise as the score instead of their own scores. ReAP's risk head ran on our world model's latent, and our VLA-Corrector verifier works on pooled image statistics instead of their 54 features with the arm state. The drafter of SURVEYOR [@surveyor2026] is not released, and REMORA's progress atoms are written by hand. These are failures of our variants, and we do not claim that the authors' methods fail.

Our inference engine matters as well. vla.cpp samples sequentially and has no batching, so adaptive chunking pays for 20 full passes, while the paper's 106 ms was measured with batching on an A800. For PACT-VLA, the time of the check is projected rather than measured. Finally, several tests were offline only: planning ranked recorded chunks, and chunking measured smoothness on recorded episodes. None of these ran in closed loop on the arm.

Table 4: Each failed or not-proven paper, whose method failed, what we measured, and what on our side may explain it.

| failed paper | whose method | our result | what may be on our side |
|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | authors' | planning rank 0.60, action-only 0.68–0.90 | one short task, 157 episodes, one seed; offline ranking only |
| DINO-WM | authors' | planning rank 0.61, action-only 0.68–0.90 | same data as LeWorldModel; one training |
| RoboJEPA | authors' | 11.8 % below copy-last, bar 15 % | near miss; the 1B model reached 15.8 % on 30 clips only |
| H-JEPA | outside its regime | progress ρ −0.73, bar −0.80; false stalls 52 % | our task is outside the paper's regime |
| HWM | not proven | not proven: high level worse at 2 s | 0.6 h of one task against their 130 h |
| ContactGuard | our variant | AUROC 0.70, current latent 0.82 | mixed policies and 2 cameras against one policy and 3 cameras |
| FAIL-Detect and SAFE | our variant | 22.7 % of successes flagged at α 5 % | our score, not theirs; 22 successes for calibration |
| ReAP | our variant | 3 of 8 failures caught | their risk head on our world model's latent, not their features; 8 failures |
| VLA-Corrector | our variant | 2 of 8, AUROC 0.59 | our features, not their 54; 8 failures |
| Robometer | authors' | AUROC 0.818, length 0.938 | zero-shot only; ArmnetBench failures run to the time limit (on our rollouts length is near chance) |
| SURVEYOR | our variant | threshold 1.249 against 0.20; 3 of 8 | their drafter not released; 8 failures |
| REMORA | our variant | readout flips 0.70 of the per-frame readout | hand-written progress atoms |
| Adaptive chunking | authors' (latency: our engine) | 4,751 ms per decision; length 2 in transport and at the grasp | sequential engine, no batching; task at ceiling for our policy |
| PACT-VLA | our engine | 37 ticks of delay against 8 | check time projected; sequential engine |
| Real-Time Chunking | not proven | not proven: 71–73 % smoother, no arm run | offline smoothness only |
| Horizon × depth | not proven | not proven: 0.78, bar 0.70 | one policy, one task; arm test not run |

## 8. Limitations

Our data covers one short task family, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, with one arm, two cameras and about {{tel_min}} minutes of demonstrations, so it says nothing about long-horizon tasks or varied scenes. There are few failures to test the monitors on, {{ro_p_fail}} in the main set of {{ro_p_eps}}. Many methods are our own variants rather than the authors' code end to end (Table 4), and the planning and chunking tests ran offline, never in closed loop on the arm. A single operator ran the arm and labelled the outcomes without being blind to them, many tests use small samples and a single training seed, and few results come with a confidence interval; a bootstrap over episodes for the baseline comparisons could be run for only {{bs_have}} of {{b_n}}, because for the others the per-episode rows were not stored or a re-run did not reproduce the stored result. Two VLA results have no numeric bar and rest on 10 trials. The bars are ours, not the papers'. We chose the papers from the stack we run, so the rates describe these papers and not the field. Finally, our own mistakes are possible.

## 9. Related work

Two works evaluate policies on the same arm: a real-world VLA benchmark with a taxonomy of failures and recoveries [@2606.08881], and ArmnetBench, a fleet of SO-101 cells that evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, vla-eval runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and another benchmark studies the quality of demonstrations [@2610.18869]. These works rank policies. We instead take the claim a paper makes about its method and test it against a bar fixed in advance, on one cheap rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its authors note that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one verdict per claim on one rig, with the data behind it and the reason we think it failed.

## 10. Conclusion

We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, with under ${{cost_round}} of cloud GPU. {{c_paper_repro}} held, and {{t_c_auth}} failed with the authors' method on our data. The most useful part was the cheapest baseline: the same world model could pass one baseline and fail another, and {{b_lost}} of the {{b_n}} methods we compared with one lost to a signal we had for free, although only {{bs_loses}} of these losses have an interval below zero, and only LeWorldModel's is a clean case with the authors' method. Our advice to anyone re-testing a paper on a cheap rig is to run that baseline first, on their own data, with the bar fixed before the test. Everything is public, and every number can be rebuilt from the repository. Authors who think a failure here is ours (Table 4) are welcome to tell us, and we will correct it.

